profile: ext_wikilinks=on
# Wikilinks

Setting: `ext_wikilinks`.

A wikilink is a target between double brackets, as in Obsidian.

``` example
See [[Backlinks]].
```

A `|` separates the target from an alias, which is the text shown.

``` example
See [[Backlinks|the other direction]].
```

A `!` in front makes it an embed, as it makes a link an image.

``` example
![[diagram.png]]
```

## Where it opens and closes

A wikilink opens at a `[` that comes directly after another `[`, and closes
at the first `]]` on the same line. A single `]` is part of the target.

``` example
[[a]b]]
```

It does not cross a line break. One that is not closed by the end of its
line is text.

``` example
[[a
b]]
```

## Target and alias

The region is split at its first `|`. Both parts are kept as written:
nothing is trimmed, a backslash is not decoded, and the alias is not parsed
as inline content.

``` example
[[a|*b*]]
```

An empty target is not a wikilink.

``` example
[[]] and [[|b]]
```

## What it changes in djot

A `[` at the start of a link's text is read as a wikilink opener. So a link
whose text begins with a bracket reads differently.

``` example
[[1]](u)
```

In a table, the row is split into cells first, so a `|` inside a wikilink
ends the cell.

## Output

A wikilink renders as the link it stands for: the target is the `href`, and
the alias, or the target when there is none, is the text. An embed renders
as an image. The tree keeps a node of its own, with the target, the alias
and whether it is an embed.

